"This course's five-elements unit described the generating cycle as a mother-child relationship between elements, and its organs unit assigned each element a specific pair of organs. Put together, they describe something Chinese medicine calls \"mother and child conditions affecting each other\" — disease traveling along the generating cycle from one organ to its neighbor, in either direction, rather than staying confined to wherever it started.": "3cb945f97a8a",
"This course's five-elements unit described the generating cycle as a mother-child relationship between elements, and its organs unit assigned each element a specific pair of organs. Put together, they describe something Chinese medicine calls \"mother and child conditions affecting each other\" — disease traveling along the generating cycle from one organ to its neighbor, in either direction, rather than staying confined to wherever it started. A disease can move \"from mother to child,\" the pattern already covered in this course's five-elements unit: a weak kidney (water) failing to nourish the liver (wood), so liver-type symptoms appear even though the kidney is the organ actually running low. It can also move the other way, \"from child to mother\": a liver under sustained strain drawing so heavily on the kidney's support that kidney-type symptoms — lower back weakness, for instance — begin to appear as a secondary effect of a problem that started in the liver. What makes this worth its own lesson, rather than being folded into the earlier generating-cycle lesson, is the diagnostic habit it trains: a symptom's location is not automatically where the underlying problem is located. A doctor who sees kidney-type symptoms doesn't stop at the kidney — the five-element map gives a short, specific list of neighboring organs worth checking first, turning what could be a wide-open guessing game into a small number of well-reasoned possibilities.": "191d22cbf5f2",
